// Wonder Lab physical science data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// 48 facts across 8 sections.
const PHYSICAL = [{"id":"light-1","section":"light","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Light travels 186,282 miles every second. Sunlight takes 8 minutes 20 seconds to reach you, so the sun you see is always the sun of eight minutes ago. The speed was first measured by timing the moons of Jupiter, and now by bouncing lasers off mirrors.","more":"The meter is now defined using this speed, rather than the speed being measured in meters. So light's speed cannot come out wrong. Everything else is measured against it."},{"id":"light-2","section":"light","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"White light is every color together. A prism spreads it out because each color bends by a slightly different amount. Newton then did the part people forget: he put a second prism in the way and put the colors back into white light.","tryit":"Fill a glass with water. Stand it on a windowsill in direct sun. Put a white sheet of paper on the floor beside it. Then hunt for the rainbow."},{"id":"light-3","section":"light","cat":"howworks","kind":"worked","text":"Air scatters short waves much more strongly than long ones. So blue light bounces around the sky while red comes straight through. That is why the sky is blue. At sunset the light travels through far more air. The blue is scattered away entirely, and you get red.","tryit":"Put a few drops of milk in a glass of water and shine a flashlight through it from the side. The water looks bluish from the side and orange from the end."},{"id":"light-4","section":"light","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Light bounces off a mirror at exactly the angle it arrived, like a ball off a wall. That single rule explains every mirror, periscope and reflector you have ever used.","tryit":"Shine a flashlight at a mirror in a dark room with a little dust or steam in the air so the beam shows. Change the angle and watch the reflection change by the same amount."},{"id":"light-5","section":"light","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Light bends when it crosses from air into water. That is why a pencil in a glass looks broken. It is why a pool is always deeper than it looks. The same bend is why lenses work at all.","tryit":"Put a coin in the bottom of an empty mug and back away until the rim just hides it. Without moving, have someone pour water in. The coin comes into view."},{"id":"light-6","section":"light","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Mixing light and mixing paint are opposites. Red, green and blue light together make white. Red, green and blue paint together make a muddy brown, because each paint is taking colors away rather than adding them.","tryit":"Put a drop of water on a phone screen showing a white picture and look closely. You will see the red, green and blue stripes the screen is really made of."},{"id":"electric-1","section":"electric","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Rubbing two things together moves tiny charges from one to the other, leaving one positive and one negative. They then pull toward each other. That is static, and it is the same force that holds every atom in your body together.","tryit":"Rub a balloon on your hair and hold it near a thin stream of water from a faucet. The water bends toward it."},{"id":"electric-2","section":"electric","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Electricity only flows round a complete loop. Break the loop anywhere and everything stops — which is what a switch is, and why one dead bulb used to kill a whole string of Christmas lights.","tryit":"With a battery, a wire and a small bulb, find every arrangement that lights it. There are fewer than you expect."},{"id":"electric-3","section":"electric","cat":"howworks","kind":"worked","text":"What flows in a wire is electrons — far too small to see. We know the amount flowing because a current pushes a magnet by a measurable amount, and that push is what an ammeter reads.","more":"Nobody has watched an electron go down a wire. What is measured is the effect, every time, consistently, and the amount is worked out from it."},{"id":"electric-4","section":"electric","cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"A lightning bolt carries around 30,000 amps and heats the air in its path to roughly 50,000°F — about five times hotter than the surface of the sun. Thunder is that air exploding outwards.","more":"The current is measured by instruments on towers that get struck deliberately. The temperature is worked out from the colors in the light."},{"id":"electric-5","section":"electric","cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"A battery makes electricity out of a chemical reaction. It needs two different metals with something wet in between. The first one was a stack of copper and zinc discs, with brine-soaked cardboard between them. You can build the same thing with coins.","tryit":"Push a galvanised nail and a copper coin into a lemon, not touching. Touch both with your tongue. That faint tingle is a working battery."},{"id":"electric-6","section":"electric","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Metals let electricity through; glass, rubber, dry wood and air stop it. That is the whole reason wires are copper wrapped in plastic. Water is a poor conductor when truly pure, and a good one the moment anything is dissolved in it.","more":"So the rule is never about water exactly. It is about the salt and minerals in real water, and in you."},{"id":"magnets-1","section":"magnets","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Every magnet has two ends and you cannot get one on its own. Snap a bar magnet in half and you do not get a north piece and a south piece — you get two smaller magnets, each with both.","tryit":"Break a cheap flexible fridge magnet strip in two and test both halves against a compass. Each still has both ends."},{"id":"magnets-2","section":"magnets","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"A compass needle does not point at the North Pole. It points at the magnetic pole, which is somewhere else entirely, and the difference can be tens of degrees depending where you are standing. Maps print the correction for that spot.","more":"Sailors and pilots have to apply it. Get it wrong over a long distance and you end up somewhere else."},{"id":"magnets-3","section":"magnets","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Electricity and magnetism are the same thing wearing different hats. Wrap wire round an iron nail, connect a battery, and the nail becomes a magnet. Disconnect it and the magnetism stops.","tryit":"Wind about fifty turns of insulated wire round a big iron nail and touch the ends to a battery briefly. Pick up paperclips. Do not leave it connected — it gets hot."},{"id":"magnets-4","section":"magnets","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The magnetic north pole moves. It has been tracked since 1831, and it is crossing the Arctic toward Siberia. Recently it moved fast enough that the world's navigation models had to be updated early.","more":"Nobody chose to move it. It is measured by survey aircraft and satellites, and it wanders."},{"id":"magnets-5","section":"magnets","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Very few things are magnetic. Iron, nickel and cobalt, and mixtures containing them. Aluminium, copper, gold and silver are not, which is a quick way to test whether something is really steel.","tryit":"Go round the house with a magnet. Try coins, cutlery, tins, the fridge, a drinks can. Sort what sticks from what does not."},{"id":"magnets-6","section":"magnets","cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Nearly every motor you own works by switching an electromagnet on and off. The moving part is always being pulled toward the next position and never quite arrives. Fans, drills, washing machines, the vibration in a phone.","more":"The trick is the switching. A magnet that simply attracts would pull once and stop."},{"id":"energy-1","section":"energy","cat":"howworks","kind":"worked","text":"Energy is never made and never destroyed — it only changes form. Nobody has ever seen an exception, and people have looked very hard, because a machine that broke this rule would be worth everything. It is the most tested idea in physics.","more":"Nobody can measure every event in the universe. So this is reasoning rather than one measurement. What we have is simpler. Every careful measurement anyone has ever made has agreed with it."},{"id":"energy-2","section":"energy","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"A pendulum never swings back higher than where you let it go. It trades height for speed on the way down and speed for height on the way up, and loses a little to the air each time.","tryit":"Hang a heavy ball on a string, hold it against your nose, and let go without pushing. Stand perfectly still. It will come back and stop just short of your face. Do not push."},{"id":"energy-3","section":"energy","cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A food Calorie is the heat needed to warm 1 kg of water by 1 degree Celsius. The numbers on food labels come from actually burning the food. It goes in a sealed metal chamber sitting in water. Then you measure how much the water warms up.","more":"The machine is called a bomb calorimeter. It is exactly as dramatic as it sounds and it is how the number on a cereal box got there."},{"id":"energy-4","section":"energy","cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"A person on a bicycle is the most efficient moving thing known. That beats any animal and any other machine. The measure is energy used per pound carried per mile. A person walking uses about three times as much.","more":"The figure comes from measuring the oxygen riders use on treadmills and converting it to energy. The bicycle wins because almost nothing is wasted holding you up."},{"id":"energy-5","section":"energy","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Stretch a rubber band quickly and it gets warmer. Let it snap back and it cools. You can feel both with your lip, which is more sensitive to temperature than your fingers.","tryit":"Touch a rubber band to your lip. Stretch it fast, touch again. Wait, let it relax, touch a third time."},{"id":"energy-6","section":"energy","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"An AA battery holds roughly the energy of a single peanut. A car's gasoline tank holds about as much energy as a hundred sticks of dynamite. Chemical fuels are extraordinarily concentrated. That is why batteries are the hard part of every electric machine.","more":"Both numbers come from the same kind of measurement — burn it, or discharge it, and count the heat."},{"id":"sound-1","section":"sound","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Sound needs something to travel through. Put a ringing alarm inside a jar and pump the air out, and the ringing fades to nothing while you can still see the hammer moving. Space is genuinely silent.","more":"The experiment is over three hundred years old and still done in classrooms. Watching a bell ring with no sound coming out is hard to forget."},{"id":"sound-2","section":"sound","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Sound moves about 767 mph through air — roughly a mile every five seconds. Light is effectively instant by comparison, which is why you can measure how far away a storm is.","tryit":"When you see lightning, count seconds until the thunder. Divide by five and that is how many miles away it struck."},{"id":"sound-3","section":"sound","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Sound travels more than four times faster in water than in air, and faster still in steel. Whales use this. So did people listening for trains by putting an ear to the rail.","tryit":"Ask someone to tap the far end of a long metal railing or a table while you press your ear to the near end. Then listen through the air. The tap through the metal arrives first."},{"id":"sound-4","section":"sound","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Pitch is how many times a second something vibrates. People hear from about 20 vibrations a second up to about 20,000. Children hear the high end; adults lose it steadily, which is why some ringtones are inaudible to teachers.","tryit":"Run a fingernail slowly along a comb, then fast. Same comb, same teeth, different pitch — only the rate changed."},{"id":"sound-5","section":"sound","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Everything has a note it prefers to vibrate at. Hit it and it rings at that note; sing that note at it and it starts vibrating on its own. That is why a wine glass can be shattered by a voice, and why bridges have to be designed with it in mind.","tryit":"Wet a finger and run it slowly round the rim of a wine glass until it sings. Add water and the note drops."},{"id":"sound-6","section":"sound","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"An echo is sound that has gone somewhere and come back. Time the round trip, halve it, multiply by the speed of sound, and you have measured a distance without touching it. Ships find the seabed this way and bats find moths.","tryit":"Shout at a big flat wall from a long way off and count. Anything under about a fifth of a second and your ears merge the echo with the shout."},{"id":"heat-1","section":"heat","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Heat only ever moves from hotter to colder, never the other way round on its own. A fridge does not make cold — it takes heat out of the inside and dumps it out of the back. Put your hand behind a fridge and feel it.","tryit":"Feel the back or underneath of your fridge. That warmth is the heat that used to be in your food."},{"id":"heat-2","section":"heat","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"A metal spoon feels colder than a wooden one lying next to it, even though a thermometer says they are exactly the same temperature. Metal carries heat away from your finger fast. Your skin does not measure temperature — it measures how quickly heat is leaving.","tryit":"Leave a metal spoon and a wooden one in the same drawer overnight, then touch both. Then check them with a thermometer and see that you were wrong."},{"id":"heat-3","section":"heat","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Warm air is thinner than cold air, so it rises and cold air slides in underneath. That single fact drives every breeze, every cloud, every hot-air balloon and the draft under your door.","tryit":"Cut a paper spiral, hang it by a thread over a radiator or lamp, and watch it turn. Nothing is blowing on it."},{"id":"heat-4","section":"heat","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Almost everything shrinks when it freezes. Water expands — by about nine percent — which is why ice floats, why pipes burst and why the top of a pond freezes while fish carry on underneath.","more":"If water behaved like everything else, ice would sink. Ponds would freeze from the bottom. Very little would survive a winter in them."},{"id":"heat-5","section":"heat","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Evaporating water takes heat with it. That is why you feel cold getting out of a bath. It is why sweating works. It is why a wet cloth round a bottle cools the drink inside on a hot day.","tryit":"Wet one finger and hold both hands up in front of a fan or an open window. Only one of them gets cold."},{"id":"heat-6","section":"heat","cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"There is a bottom to cold. At −459.67°F nothing has any heat left to give up, and nothing can be colder. There is no matching top — you can always add more heat.","more":"Nobody has ever reached it. Laboratories have got to within a few billionths of a degree. Each step closer takes enormously more effort than the last."},{"id":"forces-1","section":"forces","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"A thing that is moving keeps moving in a straight line unless something pushes it. Nothing on earth demonstrates this well, because friction and air are always pushing back. On ice, or in space, you can see it plainly.","tryit":"Put a card on a glass and a coin on the card. Flick the card sideways hard. The coin does not come with it — it drops straight into the glass."},{"id":"forces-2","section":"forces","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Friction is what stops everything. It turns movement into heat, which is why brake discs glow and why rubbing your hands warms them. Rougher surfaces make more of it, but so do some very smooth ones — two flat sheets of glass grip surprisingly hard.","tryit":"Lay a book on a table and lift one end slowly until the book slides. Do it with a shoe, a coin, an eraser. The angle each one slides at is a measurement of its friction."},{"id":"forces-3","section":"forces","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Heavy things do not fall faster than light things. On the last Apollo 15 moonwalk, David Scott held out a geology hammer and a falcon feather and let go of both. With no air in the way they hit the dust together. The camera was running.","more":"Galileo argued this without being able to prove it properly, because he could never get the air out of the way. It took a television camera on the moon to settle it in front of everybody."},{"id":"forces-4","section":"forces","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"The reason a feather falls slower than a hammer on earth is air. Air pushes back harder the faster you fall and the more of you is spread out. When the push equals your weight you stop speeding up. A skydiver levels off at about 120 mph.","tryit":"Drop two identical sheets of paper, one flat and one screwed into a ball. Same paper, same weight, completely different fall."},{"id":"forces-5","section":"forces","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A lever trades distance for force. Push the far end of a long bar a long way and the near end moves a short way with much more force. Scissors, nutcrackers, wheelbarrows, your own forearm and a seesaw are all the same machine.","tryit":"Try to crack a walnut in your hand. Then put it in the nutcracker right at the hinge, and then right at the tip. Feel where it is easiest."},{"id":"forces-6","section":"forces","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Every push comes with an equal push the other way. A rocket does not push against the air — it throws gas out of the back, and the gas pushes the rocket forward. That is why rockets work better in space, where nothing is in the way.","tryit":"Thread a straw onto a long string, tape a blown-up balloon to the straw, and let go. It will fly the length of the room."},{"id":"matter-1","section":"matter","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Water is the one substance you meet as a solid, a liquid and a gas in an ordinary day — ice, water, steam. Everything else in your house is stuck in one state unless you do something drastic to it.","tryit":"Put ice in a pan and heat it slowly with a thermometer in it. The temperature stops climbing while the ice is melting, then starts again. Watch for the pause."},{"id":"matter-2","section":"matter","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Water does not always boil at 212°F. In Denver, a mile up, it boils at about 202°F, because there is less air pressing down on it. Food takes longer to cook there, and packet instructions say so.","more":"On a high enough mountain you can boil water that is not hot enough to make tea properly."},{"id":"matter-3","section":"matter","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Whether something floats depends on how heavy it is for its size, not on how heavy it is. A steel ship floats because most of it is air. A steel bolt does not.","tryit":"Drop a ball of foil into water, then flatten the same foil into a boat shape. Same metal, same weight, opposite result."},{"id":"matter-4","section":"matter","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Air weighs something. At sea level it presses on you at 14.7 pounds on every square inch, from all directions at once — a few tons over your whole body. You do not notice because it pushes from inside too.","tryit":"Fill a glass to the brim with water, slide a card over the top, hold it and turn the glass upside down. Let go of the card. Air pressure holds the water in."},{"id":"matter-5","section":"matter","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Cornflour and water is a liquid you can run across. Hit it and it goes hard; leave your hand there and you sink. It behaves this way because the grains jam together when pushed suddenly and slide when pushed slowly.","tryit":"Mix cornflour with water until it pours like cream. Punch it fast, then press slowly. Same bowl, two completely different substances."},{"id":"matter-6","section":"matter","cat":"howworks","kind":"worked","text":"Atoms are too small to see with light. They are smaller than the waves light is made of, so no ordinary microscope will ever show one. The images that do exist were made another way. An extremely fine needle is dragged across a surface and the bumps are recorded.","more":"The first of those images was made in 1981. In 1989 a team pushed 35 individual atoms into place to spell out three letters, and photographed them. That picture is real, and it is not a photograph in the usual sense."}];
globalThis.PHYSICAL = PHYSICAL;
